# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q11696> ,
              <https://history.house.gov/Institution/Presidents-Coinciding/Presidents-Coinciding/> ,
              <https://www.whitehouse.gov/administration/donald-j-trump/> ,
              <https://www.usa.gov/presidents> ,
              <https://millercenter.org/president> ,
              <https://sv.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lista_%C3%B6ver_USA:s_presidenter> ,
              <https://frageladan.isof.se/faqs/24030> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ,
                       <#review-4> ,
                       <#review-5> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled, from which a provenance report was generated. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them, to the dossier or the report, or to findings listed below, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier and ran its Wikidata checks."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators)."@en ,
                 "1. Wikidata (query 1): every non-deprecated P39 (position held) statement whose value is President of the United States (Q11696), with its P1545 (series ordinal), P580 (start time) and P582 (end time) qualifiers, its rank and the English and Swedish labels, was fetched as CSV from the Wikidata Query Service. The result also holds fictional presidents (characters from films, comics and television, among them \"Quentin Trembley\" with the ordinal \"8½\"), which have no numeric ordinal or are not humans. Query 2 therefore restricted the statements to those with a P1545 qualifier on items that are instances of human (P31 Q5) and fetched every English, Swedish and multilingual (mul) label and alias of those people. The rows with a whole-number ordinal are 47 statements, 1 to 47, on 45 items: Grover Cleveland (Q35171) holds two statements (22 and 24) and Donald Trump (Q22686) two (45 and 47, the latter of preferred rank and without an end time)."@en ,
                 "2. Official US government check: the White House's former page listing all presidents (https://www.whitehouse.gov/about-the-white-house/presidents/) answered 404. USA.gov's page on the presidents (https://www.usa.gov/presidents, last updated March 2, 2026) states that Donald John Trump is the 47th and current president, sworn in on January 20, 2025, and links, for past presidents, to the table \"Presidents, Vice Presidents, & Coinciding Sessions of Congress\" of the House of Representatives' Office of the Historian. That table was downloaded (query 6) and parsed: it numbers 47 presidencies, Grover Cleveland as No. 22 and No. 24 and Donald J. Trump as No. 45 and No. 47 (\"Jan. 20, 2025–present\"). The White House's page on the current president (query 7) heads it \"45th & 47th President of the United States\"."@en ,
                 "3. Independent check: the Miller Center of Public Affairs (University of Virginia) list of presidents (https://millercenter.org/president, query 8) gives a number, a first and last year and a name for each president. (Britannica's table of presidents was tried first and answered 403 to the research environment: curl -sL -A \"solid-memo deck research (https://github.com/antwika/solid-memo)\" -o britannica.html -w \"%{http_code} %{url_effective} %{size_download}\\n\" https://www.britannica.com/topic/Presidents-of-the-United-States-1846696; it was not used.)"@en ,
                 "4. Swedish: the Swedish Wikipedia list \"Lista över USA:s presidenter\" was fetched with its revision id through the MediaWiki API (query 9). Its rows link each president's Swedish article; every Swedish name on the cards is the Wikidata sv label (or, for George Washington, Ronald Reagan, George W. Bush, Barack Obama, Donald Trump and Joe Biden, who have no sv label, the mul label) and equals the title of the linked Swedish article. Its introduction uses the ordinal abbreviations \"22:a och 24:e … 45:e och 47:e\", the pattern used on the Swedish fronts: \"USA:s 22:a president\". The rule is Språkrådet's (Frågelådan, https://frageladan.isof.se/faqs/24030, query 12): a numeral, a colon and the last letter of the spelled-out ordinal (\"1:a, 2:a, 3:e, 4:e och så vidare\"). The Swedish ordinal abbreviation is therefore :a for numbers ending in 1 or 2 (första, andra, tjugoförsta …) except 11 and 12 (elfte, tolfte), and :e for all others."@en ,
                 "5. Cross-check: the script compare.py (query 10) parsed the saved Wikidata CSVs, the House table, the Miller Center list and the Swedish Wikipedia list, and compared for every number the first and last year in office; it found no disagreement. Its rules: Wikidata = the P39 statements of query 1 whose P1545 is a whole number, start and end year taken from P580 and P582 (no P582 = \"present\"); House table = the table rows grouped under each \"No.\" (a row with an empty No. cell continues the presidency above), start year from the first row's dates of service and end year from the last row's (\"present\" if it has none); Miller Center = the \"president-number\", \"inauguration-date\" and \"date-ended\" fields of each entry (the end year is not compared where Wikidata has no end); Swedish Wikipedia = the last four characters of \"tillträde\" and \"frånträde\" in the {{Tabell befattningshavare}} block with that \"nummer\" (wiki links and <ref> notes removed). The script compares years only; that each number belongs to the same person in every source, and the name forms, were checked by reading the parsed rows side by side. The script wrote its parsed readings to compare.json, and the evidence on every card (the \"says\" texts of the Wikidata, House, Miller Center and Swedish Wikipedia evidence) was generated from those parsed fields by make_dossier.py, not typed by hand; the readings of the White House and USA.gov pages were added by hand. The scripts themselves are not published with the deck; the rules above are what they do. The Miller Center's end year for the current term, which the script does not compare (Wikidata has no end), was read by hand: it is an error on that page (see quality control). The names were compared by reading: the House table uses formal forms (\"William H. Taft\", \"Richard M. Nixon\", \"Gerald R. Ford\", \"James Earl “Jimmy” Carter\", \"William J. Clinton\", \"Donald J. Trump\", \"Joseph R. Biden Jr.\"), the Miller Center \"William Harrison\" and \"William Taft\"; all name the same person as the Wikidata item. The cards use the Wikidata English label, the name by which each president is commonly known, except for Franklin D. Roosevelt, whose English label is the full \"Franklin Delano Roosevelt\": the card uses \"Franklin D. Roosevelt\" (an English and a mul alias of the item, the form of the House table, the Miller Center and the Swedish label)."@en ,
                 "6. Cards: front \"<ordinal> president of the United States\" / \"USA:s <ordinal> president\" (English ordinals 1st, 2nd, 3rd, 4th, 11th, 12th, 13th, 21st …); back: the name; back note: \"President <first year>–<last year>\" in both languages, from the years of the P580 and P582 qualifiers, with the months for the two presidencies that began and ended in the same year (William Henry Harrison and James A. Garfield); the notes do not say how a presidency ended (death, assassination or resignation), so that no card implies anything about the others, and, for Cleveland and Trump, the other number and term. Study direction front to back only, because Cleveland and Trump each answer two cards."@en ,
                 "7. Machine checks: for every card the builder checked against live Wikidata that the item holds P39 Q11696 and has the English and Swedish name of the back as a label or alias (in that language or mul). The builder's checks therefore confirm only that each person held the office and the English and Swedish name forms; they would not notice two cards with their names swapped. The number on each front and the years in each note are qualifiers of the P39 statement, which the builder's checks cannot reach; they rest on query 1 and the comparison in step 5 (Wikidata, the House table, the Miller Center and the Swedish Wikipedia) and are recorded in each card's evidence. The built deck was validated with the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators (validate_sources.ts)."@en ,
                 "Selection: Every presidency of the United States from the first (George Washington, 1789) to the current one (Donald Trump's second term, since 20 January 2025), as of 4 October 2026: 47 cards. A presidency is a numbered term of continuous service, the convention of the US government (the House of Representatives' table, the White House, USA.gov), of Wikidata and of the Miller Center: a president who served two non-consecutive terms is counted twice, so Grover Cleveland is the 22nd and the 24th president and Donald Trump the 45th and the 47th, and the 47 presidencies were held by 45 people. Consecutive terms of the same president are one presidency (Franklin D. Roosevelt's four terms are the 32nd). Left out: acting presidents and anyone who exercised the powers of the office temporarily under the 25th Amendment (not numbered by any source), vice presidents, presidents of the Continental Congress or under the Articles of Confederation (before the Constitution) and the Confederate States, and the fictional presidents that the Wikidata query also returned. The current presidency is the cut-off: the note says \"since 2025\"."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The only content source is Wikidata, whose structured data is CC0: the selection of presidencies, their numbers, the years in the notes and the English and Swedish names all come from it, so the deck is CC0 1.0 like the rest of the library. The House of Representatives' table, the White House and USA.gov (US government works, not under copyright in the US), the Miller Center (all rights reserved) and the Swedish Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0) were used for verification only: they confirmed facts that the Wikidata data gave, and no wording, table layout or selection was copied from them. The selection is the complete set of numbered presidencies, which any list of presidents shares because it is the complete set, not a choice taken from a list. The fronts and notes are the compiler's own wording of facts."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks: every card's number, name and years against Wikidata, the House of Representatives' table, the Miller Center and the Swedish Wikipedia; the current president against the White House and USA.gov; the Swedish names and ordinals against Wikidata sv labels and the Swedish Wikipedia."@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 47 cards, the sources and the deck metadata."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Wikidata, the House table, the Miller Center and the Swedish Wikipedia agree on the number, the person and the first and last year of all 47 presidencies, and the White House and USA.gov confirm that Donald Trump is the 47th and current president. No Wikidata check was skipped. The differences found are in name forms, in Wikidata's modelling (fictional presidents, missing sv labels) and one error on the Miller Center page; none affects a card."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 47 cards, checked against the UCSB American Presidency Project (not used by the author), the White House and news of October 2026."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors in the cards: the order, every name and every first and last year agree with the UCSB American Presidency Project, and Donald Trump is still in office on 4 October 2026. Two suggestions, both applied: the death-in-office remark on William Henry Harrison's card only was removed, and the method now says plainly what the builder's Wikidata checks do and do not cover."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 47 cards, the title, description and keywords, and the built TTL."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors on the cards: every card text is tagged @en or @sv, the English and Swedish ordinals and Swedish month names are right. The untagged keywords are a limitation of the builder, outside the dossier, and are left to the builder's owner. The description now names the House source correctly and its Swedish wording is more idiomatic; the Harrison note was made consistent; the Garfield and Bush name forms were kept as sourced."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 6 sources and their licence pages, the evidence of 16 cards against freshly fetched pages, and the method, queries and licensing text."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Licensing and attribution are compliant (CC0, Wikidata the only content source). The comparison rules of the cross-check scripts are now spelled out in method step 5, the Miller Center evidence on the 47th card quotes only the page, the USA.gov licence evidence cites its real policies page, the Franklin D. Roosevelt alias is described as English and mul, and the Swedish ordinal rule is now sourced to Språkrådet."@en .

<#review-4>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-04T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Re-check of changed cards and a sample (facts, language, documentation)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The three cards changed in round 3 (president-9, president-32, president-47) and every third card (1, 4, 7 … 46), 19 cards in all, against freshly fetched sources; the sources' usedFor texts and the method."@en ,
                 "Outcome: All 19 checked cards match live Wikidata, the House Office of the Historian table and the Miller Center; round 3's fixes are confirmed. The USA.gov source's usedFor misread \"45 former U.S. presidents\" and is corrected; method step 7 no longer repeats itself. No card changed."@en .

<#review-5>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Version 2: title, description and keywords in Korean, German, Spanish and French (AI)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: the deck’s title, description and keywords, translated by an AI agent from the English (the Swedish settling what was ambiguous) into Korean, German, Spanish and French, in the words the app itself uses, and checked by a second AI agent that translated every text back into English and fixed what had drifted; the release compared by a script with version 1, which found the new title, description and keywords and the release’s own metadata the only changes, and checked by the library checker; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: the title, description and keywords are in Korean, German, Spanish and French as well; no card changed."@en .

